{"name":"Brown Bunny","hex":"#c2ae93","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"},{"term":"bunny","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rabbit, especially a juvenile one.","A bunny girl: a nightclub waitress who wears a costume having rabbit ears and tail."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Easy or unchallenging."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bunny"}],"entries":[{"title":"The Brown Bunny","blocks":[{"text":"The Brown Bunny is a 2003 independent, erotic, romance, and road drama film written, directed, produced, photographed, and edited by Vincent Gallo. Starring Gallo and Chloë Sevigny, it tells the story of a motorcycle racer on a cross-country drive who is haunted by memories of his former lover. It was photographed with handheld 16 mm cameras in various locations throughout the United States, including New Hampshire, Massachusetts, Ohio, Missouri, Utah, Nevada, and California."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/4f/Brown_bunny_post.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Brown_Bunny"},{"title":"Rabbit","blocks":[{"text":"Rabbits, or bunnies, are small mammals in the family Leporidae, which is in the order Lagomorpha. They are familiar throughout the world as a small herbivore, a prey animal, a domesticated form of livestock, and a pet, having a widespread effect on ecologies and cultures. The most widespread rabbit genera are Oryctolagus and Sylvilagus. The former, Oryctolagus, includes the European rabbit, Oryctolagus cuniculus, which is the ancestor of the hundreds of breeds of domestic rabbit and has been introduced on every continent except Antarctica. The latter, Sylvilagus, includes over 13 wild rabbit species, among them the cottontails and tapetis. Wild rabbits not included in Oryctolagus and Sylvilagus include several species of limited distribution, including the pygmy rabbit, volcano rabbit, and Sumatran striped rabbit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Oryctolagus_cuniculus_Rcdo.jpg/330px-Oryctolagus_cuniculus_Rcdo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabbit"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f9/The_Brown_Bunny.jpg/250px-The_Brown_Bunny.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/f9/The_Brown_Bunny.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f9/The_Brown_Bunny.jpg/250px-The_Brown_Bunny.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Brown_Bunny.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Meyer_Zeit-Vertreib_2_Tafel_032.jpg/500px-Meyer_Zeit-Vertreib_2_Tafel_032.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Meyer_Zeit-Vertreib_2_Tafel_032.jpg/500px-Meyer_Zeit-Vertreib_2_Tafel_032.jpg?utm_